{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 biographical drama film directed by david fincher, written by acid screenwriter jesse eisenberg, and produced by scott rudin, dana bruno, and michael de la oueijy. the film is based on the book \"the accidental billionaire\" by ben mezrich, which tells the story of the founding of the social networking site facebook.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, a brilliant and ambitious college student at harvard university. zuckerberg is portrayed as a complex character, both brilliant and arrogant, who is driven by a desire to create a social network that will change the world. the film also stars andrew garfield as edward saverin, zuckerberg's best friend and business partner, and joshua jackson as sean parker, a brilliant programmer who helps zuckerberg develop facebook.\n\nthe film begins with the story of zuckerberg and his relationship with saverin, who is portrayed as a more down-to-earth and empathetic character. the two friends meet in high school and quickly become close, bonding over their shared love of computer programming and their desire to create something new and innovative.\n\nas they enter harvard university, zuckerberg and saverin are determined to make a name for themselves and create a social network that will revolutionize the way people interact with each other. they recruit parker, who is portrayed as a charismatic and confident character, to help them develop the site.\n\nthe film follows the story of facebook's early days, from its humble beginnings as a small social network to its rapid growth and success. the film also explores the personal and professional relationships between the characters, including zuckerberg's relationship with his girlfriend, erin andrews, and his rivalry with parker, who is portrayed as a more laid-back and easy-going character.\n\none of the strengths of the film is its ability to balance the personal and professional aspects of the characters' lives. the film is both a biographical drama and a social commentary, exploring the impact of technology on society and the consequences of ambition and greed.\n\nthe film features a talented cast of actors, including jesse eisenberg, andrew garfield, and joshua jackson, who all deliver strong performances. the film also features a number of memorable supporting characters, including armie hammer as cambridge connecticut student erin andrews, and max milian as divya narendra.\n\nthe film's direction by david fincher is also noteworthy, as he brings a level of sophistication and nuance to the film's portrayal of the characters and their relationships. the film's cinematography is also noteworthy, with a number of memorable shots that capture the energy and excitement of the characters' experiences.\n\none of the weaknesses of the film is its portrayal of the female characters, who are often relegated to the background and portrayed as minor characters. this is particularly evident in the film's portrayal of erin andrews, who is portrayed as a somewhat one-dimensional character.\n\noverall, the social network is a compelling and thought-provoking film that explores the personal and professional relationships between the characters and the impact of technology on society. the film features a talented cast of actors, memorable supporting characters, and a number of memorable shots that capture the energy and excitement of the characters' experiences. despite its weaknesses, the film is a must-see for anyone interested in technology, business, and social commentary.\n\nrating: 4.5/5", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.045232724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0016868184}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.6293654e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.18040486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00012385078}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.32515162}, {"token": "201", "logpro